\begin{abstract}
When the electromagnetic wave is incident on the periodic structures, in addition to the scattering field, some propagating modes that are traveling in the periodic medium could be generated. In the present paper, we study the calculation of propagating modes. We formulate the problem as a nonlinear eigenvalue problem in an unbounded periodic domain. Then we use perfectly matched layers to truncate the unbounded domain, recast the problem to a quadratic eigenvalue problem, and prove the approximation property of the truncation. Finally, we formulate the quadratic eigenvalue problem to a general eigenvalue problem, use the finite element method to discrete the truncation problem, and show numerical examples to verify theoretical results. 
\end{abstract}
%{\footnotesize{\bf Mathematics Subject Classification}(MSC2020): 35B27, 65N25, 78A40,78A50, 78M10}
{{\bf Mathematics Subject Classification}(MSC2020): 35P30, 35B27, 78M10, 47A56}\\
{{\bf Keywords:} electromagnetic wave in periodic structure, propagating mode, open waveguide, perfectly matched layer, quadratic eigenvalue}

\section{Introduction}
The scattering problem in periodic structures has long been an important topic in the numerical analysis community. Indeed, when an electromagnetic wave is incident on the periodic structures, in addition to the scattering part, there are certain propagating modes which travel along the periodic structure.
Mathematically, we consider the periodic scattering problem in $\mathbb{R}^2$. We assume that the wave number $k$ is homogeneous in $\mathbb{R}^2$ and the refractive index $\gamma(x)$ is $\Lambda$-periodic in the $x_1$ direction. That is to say,
$$\gamma(x_1+\Lambda,x_2)=\gamma(x_1,x_2),$$
and $\gamma(x_1,x_2)=1$ for $|x_2|>h_0$ for some $h_0>0$. In the following sections, we let $\Omega=\{(x_{1},x_{2})|-\frac{\Lambda}{2}<x_{1}<\frac{\Lambda}{2},x_{2}\in\mathbb{R}\}$ be the Wigner-Seitz cell of the periodic structure \cite{Lechleiter}. Then we can state the scattering problem as follows.
Given an incident wave $u^{inc}$, the governing equation of the total field $u$ is 
\begin{equation}
\Delta u + k^2 \gamma(x)u = 0, \label{Hequ}
\end{equation} 
and $u=u^{inc}+u^s+u_{prop}$, 
\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering{
{\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{figures/region/modelboundary1.eps}}
}
\caption{The description of the periodic structures and the propagating mode.}\label{pbdry}
\end{figure}
see Figure \ref{pbdry} for a picture of the problem, where $u^{inc}$ is the incident wave, $u^s$ is the scattering wave, and $u_{prop}$ is the possible propagating mode which will be defined and explained later. When $u^{inc}$ is $\alpha$-quasi-periodic, namely,
\begin{equation*}
u^{inc}(x_{1}+\Lambda,x_{2})=e^{i\alpha\Lambda}u^{inc}(x_{1},x_{2}), 
\end{equation*}
one can expect that the above equation is uniquely solvable for some $\alpha$-quasi-periodic $u\in H^{1}_{\alpha,loc}(\mathbb{R}^{2})=\{u\in H^{1}_{loc}(\mathbb{R}^{2})|u $\text{ is }$ \alpha$\text{-quasi-periodic}$\}$, which satisfies a Rayleigh propagation condition such that for some $H>0$, $H>h_{0}$, in $\Omega_{H}^{+}=\{x=(x_{1},x_{2})\in\Omega|x_{2}>H\}$
\begin{equation}
     u-u^{inc} = \sum_{n\in \mathbb{Z}}u^{+}_{n}e^{i\alpha_{n}x_{1}+i\beta_{n}(x_{2}-H)}, \label{uRay0}
\end{equation}
and in $\Omega_{H}^{-}=\{x=(x_{1},x_{2})\in\Omega|x_{2}<-H\}$
\begin{equation}
     u-u^{inc} = \sum_{n\in \mathbb{Z}}u^{-}_{n}e^{i\alpha_{n}x_{1}-i\beta_{n}(x_{2}+H)}. \label{lRay0}
\end{equation}
Here
\begin{eqnarray*}
\alpha_{n}=\alpha+\frac{2\pi}{\Lambda} n, n\in\mathbb{Z}
\end{eqnarray*}
and
\begin{eqnarray*}
\beta_{n}=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}\sqrt{k^{2}-\alpha_{n}^{2}} , &|\alpha_{n}|\leq k,\\
i\sqrt{\alpha_{n}^{2}-k^{2}}, &|\alpha_{n}|>k.
\end{array}\right.
\end{eqnarray*}
We also denote by $B_{\alpha}:=\{n\in\mathbb{Z}||\alpha_{n}|\leq k\}$, $U_{\alpha}:=\{n\in\mathbb{Z}||\alpha_{n}|> k\}$.
The uniqueness of the solution implies that $u_{prop}$ vanishes. However, as was shown in \cite{Kirsch1}, for certain $\gamma(x)$ and $k$ that satisfy our assumption, there are possibly $\alpha$s such that the corresponding Rayleigh radiating condition is not enough to guarantee the uniqueness of a solution to \eqref{Hequ}. These $\alpha$s are called propagating values, and the corresponding $u_{prop}$s are called propagating modes which destroy the uniqueness; see the detailed definition in Definition \ref{def1}.

The mathematical analysis on the above scattering problem has attracted the attention of numerous researchers, whose main concern is a suitable description of a scattering condition in the $x_{2}$ direction which guarantees the uniqueness of the scattering problem while including the propagating modes.
Starting from \cite{Fliss2}, using the limiting absorption principle with the Floquet-Bloch transformation, the characterization of propagating modes in a closed waveguide problem was performed, while \cite{Kirsch1} was the first to apply the transformation to the case of a semi-open waveguide problem to derive the suitable scattering condition, and further development in the case of scattering by locally perturbed periodic structures can be seen in \cite{Kirsch2,Kirsch3,Kirsch4,Fliss2,Fliss3,Lechleiter,Nosich}.

In the case of numerical algorithms, there has been a surge of developing reliable numerical methods for scattering by periodic/locally perturbed periodic structures, for instance, the scheme using the Floquet-Bloch transformation \cite{Lechleiter}, volume integral equation method \cite{Furuya} and boundary integral equation method with specially designed perfectly matched layer (PML) and Neumann-to-Dirichlet (NtD) operator \cite{Yu}. In addition, there has recently been work on the design of the PML method for the case of scattering by a locally perturbed periodic structure \cite{Kirsch5}, where a specially chosen contour is designed to avoid propagating values for the inverse Floquet transformation. Moreover, we note that the PML method analyzed in \cite{Kirsch5} avoided the computation of propagating values, thus the computation of propagating values and the corresponding propagating modes remains an important problem.

On the other hand, for the case of the closed periodic waveguide problem, where a Dirichlet/Neumann boundary condition on the total wave $u$ is posed in the $x_{2}$-direction, the computation of corresponding eigenmodes for $\alpha$s, are thoroughly studied. Here, the eigenmodes are given the name guided modes and are of considerable interest both mathematically \cite{Kuchment1,Kuchment2} and numerically \cite{Fliss1,Zhang3,Engstrom1}. Furthermore, in the case of the locally perturbed periodic close waveguide, \cite{Zhang2} included the numerical computation of guided waves as a necessary step. 

The major contribution of our work is thus the numerical method devoted to the computation of such propagating values in the open periodic waveguide problem by designing a suitable PML truncation from which the problem is transformed into a closed periodic waveguide problem, and well-established numerical methods can be applied. Furthermore, a novel analysis of the approximation of the truncation is thoroughly addressed.  

To be more specific, we consider the approximation analysis of the PML truncation \cite{ChenWu}, namely, the truncated solution to the scattering problem converges exponentially in terms of the PML parameter $\sigma$ and the thickness of the PML layer. This idea relies on the property that the PML truncated problem converges uniformly to the original problem. We will address this issue by a novel analysis with tools from the PML analysis as \cite{Zhang1}.

The structure of the paper is as follows, in Section \ref{sect2} we recall definition of the propagating values and truncate the problem on a bounded domain,  where the problem of nonlinearity given by the Rayleigh expansion is capsuled by the Dirichlet-to-Neumann (DtN) operator. We further analyze the basic properties of the propagating values and the analyticity of the corresponding operator in Section. %\ref{sect3}. 
In Section \ref{sect3}, we circumvent the nonlinearity of the Rayleigh expansion by considering the idea of PML truncation and introduce a PML DtN operator to arrive at a second-order eigenvalue problem. %In Section \ref{sect5}, 
we also consider the perturbation by PML truncation in terms of the perturbation of the operator and derive the exponential decaying property in the operator norm in terms of the PML parameter. In Section \ref{sect4}, we give a closer study of the multiplicity of propagating values. With all tools at hand, in Section \ref{sect5} we use the exponential decaying property of the operator perturbation to derive the exponential decaying property of the perturbation of propagating values, where a novel trace inequality is derived and the inspiration for the novel analysis in this Section comes from \cite{Ammari} for the spectral analysis of perturbation by small inclusions.
In Section \ref{sect6}, we include an analysis on the finite element approximation of the truncated problem to finish the analysis of the discrete scheme following \cite{Babuska,Boffi,Bao1}. In Section \ref{sect7}, we present some numerical experiments to demonstrate the reliability of our method.
