Note that matrices $A^{h}_{\sigma}, C^{h}_{\sigma}$ are complex symmetric and that matrix $B^{h}_{\sigma}$ is skew-symmetric. Moreover, the real part of $C^{h}_{\sigma}$ is positive definite and thus invertible.
If $\alpha \in \mathbb{C}$ is an eigenvalue of the discrete problem, there exists non-zero $\hat{x}$ satisfying,
\begin{equation*}
    \begin{bmatrix}
        A^{h}_{\sigma} & 0 \\ 
        0 & I^{h}
    \end{bmatrix}\hat{x}
    =\alpha
    \begin{bmatrix}
        -B^{h}_{\sigma} & -C^{h}_{\sigma} \\ 
        I^{h} & 0
    \end{bmatrix}\hat{x}.
\end{equation*}
Due to the invertibility of matrix $C^{h}_{\sigma}$, this expression is equivalent to
\begin{equation*}
   \begin{bmatrix}
        A^{h}_{\sigma} & 0 \\ 
        0 & C^{h}_\sigma
    \end{bmatrix}\hat{x}
    =\alpha
    \begin{bmatrix}
        -B^{h}_{\sigma} & -C^{h}_{\sigma} \\ 
        C^{h}_\sigma & 0
    \end{bmatrix}\hat{x}.
\end{equation*}
Then we have 
\begin{equation*}
    det\big(\begin{bmatrix}
        A^{h}_{\sigma} & 0 \\ 
        0 & C^{h}_\sigma
    \end{bmatrix}
    -\alpha
    \begin{bmatrix}
        -B^{h}_{\sigma} & -C^{h}_{\sigma} \\ 
        C^{h}_\sigma & 0
    \end{bmatrix}\big)= det\big(\begin{bmatrix}
        A^{h}_{\sigma} & 0 \\ 
        0 & C^{h}_\sigma
    \end{bmatrix}^T
    -\alpha
    \begin{bmatrix}
        -B^{h}_{\sigma} & -C^{h}_{\sigma} \\ 
        C^{h}_\sigma & 0
    \end{bmatrix}^T\big)=0.
\end{equation*}
Here, the superscript $T$ means the transpose of complex matrices. Since $B^h_\sigma$ is skew-symmetric, we have $(B^h_\sigma)^T=-B^h_\sigma$ and
\begin{equation*}
    det\big(\begin{bmatrix}
        A^{h}_{\sigma} & 0 \\ 
        0 & C^{h}_\sigma
    \end{bmatrix}
    +\alpha
    \begin{bmatrix}
        -B^{h}_{\sigma} & -C^{h}_{\sigma} \\ 
        C^{h}_\sigma & 0
    \end{bmatrix}\big)=0.
\end{equation*}
This means that $\alpha$ and $-\alpha$ are the eigenvalues of the discrete problem simultaneously. Then, to find the propagating values, we only need to solve the problem \eqref{disblockdiag} to find all the eigenvalues with a very small imaginary part and whose real part lies in $[0,0.5)$.
