\section{Perplexity Calculation}

Perplexity serves as a measure of a model's prediction accuracy, with lower values indicating better performance. It is calculated using the following formula:

\begin{equation}
PPL(X) = \exp \left\{ -\frac{1}{t} \sum_{i} \log p_{\theta}(x_i | x_{<i}) \right\}
\end{equation}

where $PPL(X)$ denotes the perplexity of sequence $X$, $T$ is the total number of tokens in $X$, $x_i$ is the $i$th token, $x_{<i}$ represents all tokens preceding $x_i$, and $\log p_{\theta}(x_i | x_{<i})$ is the log-likelihood of token $x_i$ given its preceding context as predicted by the model parameters $\theta$.

