Processing $s_2$ is done in a similar but slightly different way. Let $l_2$ and $r_2$ be the left and right endpoints of $s_2$, respectively. Define $e_{l2}$, $a_{l2}$, $b_{l2}$ for $l_2$, and $e_{r2}$, $a_{r2}$, $b_{r2}$ for $r_2$, similarly to the above. If $e_{r1}$ is not $e_{l2}$, then we process $s_2$ in the same way as above (i.e., replace $s_2$ by $\overline{a_{l2}b_{r2}}$). Otherwise, $e_{l2}$ is already included in the new segment for $s_1$; in this case, we replace $s_2$ by $\overline{b_{l2}b_{r2}}$ (see Fig.~\ref{fig:segment20}). Although the new segment does not fully cover the old $s_2$, the union of the new $s_1$ and the new $s_2$ still covers the union of the old $s_1$ and $s_2$. Also, both endpoints of the new $s_2$ are now in $Q_{\Delta}\cap Q^*_{\Delta}$. Following a similar argument to the above, the number of arcs of $\calA$ intersecting the new $s_2$ is at most $(5c_0+2)k$.


