Observe that in {\JS} jargon, the term \emph{keyword} indicates the entire field,
that is, the pair \emph{keywordName: keywordValue}, such as ${\qtype: \key{Tp}}$,
not just the ``keyword-name''; this can be a bit confusing.
In the rest of the paper, we use just~$K$ in order to range on keywords, whenever the distinction
$\key{IK}$-$\key{SDK}$-$\key{ADK}$ is irrelevant.

In the grammar, square brackets ``['' and ``]'', and curly brackets ``\{'' and ``\}'',
are terminal symbols, while we use
$(X\ \SEP{,})^*$ to indicate a repetition of zero or more instance of the terminal $X$ each followed
by the separator  ``,'', and we use $( E )^?$ to indicate optional $E$; we only use them in the
