Let $P$ be a $k$-dominating shortest path of $G$. For every vertex $v$ of $G$, denote by $x_v$ a vertex of $P$ closest to $v$, i.e., with $d_G(v,x_v)= d_G(v,P)\le k$. We can build a caterpillar tree $T$ from $G$ by using $P$ as the central path of $T$ and making every vertex $v$ not in $P$ adjacent in $T$ to $x_v\in P$. We show that, for every pair $x,y\in V$, $d_T(x,y)-2-2k\le d_G(x,y)\le d_T(x,y)+2k$. 
Indeed, since $P$ is a shortest path of $G$, by the triangle inequality, we get $$d_T(x,y)\le d_P(x_v,y_v)+2=  d_G(x_v,y_v)+2\le d_G(x,x_v)+d_G(x,y)+d_G(y,y_v)+2\le d_G(x,y)+2k+2.$$ We also get $$d_G(x,y)\le d_G(x,x_v)+d_G(x_v,y_v) +d_G(y_v,y)\le d_P(x_v,y_v)+2k=d_T(x_v,y_v)+2k\le d_T(x,y)+2k.$$ 
