Consider now, additionally to $k$-dominating shortest path $P$ between $s$ and $t$, an arbitrary path $Q$ connecting $s$ and $t$ in $G$. Let $v$ be an arbitrary vertex of $G$ and assume $p(v)$ is a vertex of $P$ with $d_G(v,p(v))\le k$. Assume $p(v)$ belongs to layer $L_i$ of $L(s, G):=\{L_0,L_1,\dots L_q\}$ (see Figure \ref{fig:one}).   Since $Q$ must intersect $L_i$, we have a vertex $u\in L_i\cap Q$. From the proof above about $breadth(L(s, G))\le 2k$, for $v_i=p(v)$, $d_G(v_i,u)\le 2k$ holds. Hence, we get $d_G(u,v)\le d_G(u,v_i)+d_G(v_i,v)\le 3k$. Thus, an arbitrary path $Q$ connecting $s$ and $t$ has every vertex $v$ of $G$ within distance at most $3k$. The latter implies that $s$ and $t$ form  a $3k$-dominating pair of $G$, i.e., $\dpr(G)\le 3\cdot\dsp(G)$. 
