{\sc{Note added:} }
When this paper was ready for submission to a journal, we learned about two recent interesting papers \cite{coarse-tw2,coarse-tree-width}. Authors of \cite{coarse-tree-width} prove stronger results: the $k$-path-breadth (the $k$-tree-breadth) of a graph $G$ is bounded if and only if there is an $(L,C)$-quasi-isometry (with $L$ and $C$ bounded) from $G$ to a graph $H$ with pseudo-path-width (with  pseudo-tree-width, respectively) at most $k-1$. See \cite{coarse-tree-width}    for details. The results of \cite{coarse-tw2} are similar but somehow weaker:  the $k$-path-breath (or $k$-tree-breadth) of a graph $G$ is bounded if and only if there is an $(L,C)$-quasi-isometry (with $L$ and $C$ bounded) from $G$ to a graph $H$ with path-width (or tree-width, respectively) at most $2k-1$.  See \cite{coarse-tw2} for details.
