Across all AI conferences, a clear trend emerges: the number of accepted papers has consistently increased over time. 
Figure~\ref{fig:papers_per_year} illustrates the growth in paper acceptance for major AI conferences over the past decade.
While most conferences demonstrate a steady rise in the number of published papers, a notable exception is the International Joint Conferences on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI).
Unlike others, IJCAI has stabilized its publication volume in recent years. 
We attribute this stability to strict policy, which limits authors to submitting a maximum of eight papers per year. 
This policy likely curtails excessive submissions and fosters a more balanced participation. 
