% and in the meanwhile it incurs roughly 6 times less additional space cost than the horizontal scheme. % significantly narrows the gap between theory and practice: it addresses the limitation of a horizontal scheme by significantly extending its optimal read-write tradeoff coverage. also extends the design under tiering merge policy, essentially extends the optimality results  (2) The downside of the horizontal scheme are its tight coupling with the full compaction policy that incurs higher space amplification, as well as its weaker capability in handling update-heavy workloads. To address these issues, we then present a new LSM-tree structure, named {\ourmethod}, that harnesses the strengths of both schemes. When integrated into a full system RocksDB,  {\ourmethod} shows a favorable write performance compared to the vertical scheme, %by up to 1.54 times, 
