The above results have very strong implications for algorithmic solving of the phase retrieval. In Figure \ref{fig:fig1} we show how $\phi_0$ changes as a function of $x$ for several different values of $c$. As can be seen from the figure, as $c$ increases the curves are lower and at the same time flatter in the low $x$ (overlap) regime. The value $\alpha^{(1)}\triangleq 1.7932$ is chosen so that for $c=1$ the curve is as flat as it can be without having a local maximum. This would indicate that as long as the sample complexity ratio $\alpha$ is larger than $\alpha^{(1)}$, (\ref{eq:ex1a4}) can be solved by \emph{any} descending algorithm (say, any form of the gradient descent) without having such an algorithm being trapped in a local optimum.
