\label{fig:fig8}
\end{figure}
As can be see, contour lines for $c>1$ start experiencing an unwanted boundary minimum at $x=0$  which (depending on the algorithm starting point) might be one of the (undesired) funneling points. Increasing oversampling ratio $\alpha$ is expected to remedy this. In Figure \ref{fig:fig9} we see that this is indeed the case. Figures \ref{fig:fig10} and \ref{fig:fig11} show the entire manifolds for $\alpha=1.7932$ and $\alpha=2.3$ and one can observe that for $\alpha=2.3$ the lower left corner (large $c$ and small $x$) the manifold is sufficiently lifted that only  one (desired) funneling point $(c,x)=(1,1)$ remains. Despite the fact that all the contour curves and manifold have a favorable shape for $\alpha=2.3$, one still can not make a generic conclusion that the plain gradient exhibits a phase transition phenomenon. In fact, we tested the shape of the contour curves and manifolds for much larger  $\alpha$'s and were always able to find $c>1$ such that the manifold has multiple funneling points.

