\section{Conclusion}
In this work, we introduced \name, a challenging benchmark of 235 competitive programming problems from IOI and ICPC World Finals. Top models achieve only 15.1\% and 11.4\% pass@1 rates. Our benchmark includes standard and interactive programming challenges alongside a novel self-assessment task. By incorporating human competition data, we established metrics comparing LLMs with top-tier programmers, revealing substantial room for improvement. Test-time scaling law validation confirms current models have not reached performance ceilings, suggesting promising directions for advancing LLMs' reasoning in complex programming tasks. \name aims to drive progress toward code LLMs that  reach the proficiency level of elite human competitors.
% In this work, we introduced \name, a challenging benchmark comprising 230 competitive programming problems from IOI and ICPC World Finals. Our evaluation revealed that even the most advanced models achieve only 15.1\% and 11.4\% pass@1 rates, highlighting a significant gap compared to existing benchmarks where models often exceed 50\% accuracy. The benchmark's inclusion of both standard and interactive programming challenges, alongside our novel self-assessment task, addresses critical limitations in current evaluation approaches. By incorporating human competition data, we established clear metrics for comparing LLMs against expert programmers, revealing substantial room for improvement. Our empirical validation of test-time scaling laws confirms that current models have not yet reached performance ceilings, suggesting promising directions for advancing LLMs' reasoning capabilities in complex programming tasks. Through \name, we aim to drive progress toward AI systems that can truly understand and solve challenging programming problems at levels approaching human expertise.


