\title{Fibrational perspectives on determinization of finite-state automata}
\author{Thea Li
\institute{Université Paris Cité \\ Paris, France}
\email{thea.li@etu.u-paris.fr}}
\date{}
\def\titlerunning{}
\def\authorrunning{}



\begin{document}
\maketitle

% \tableofcontents
% \newpage

\begin{abstract}
  Colcombet and Petrişan argued that automata may be usefully considered from a
  functorial perspective, introducing a general notion of "$\mc{V}$-automaton"
  based on functors into $\mc{V}$.
  This enables them to recover different standard notions of automata by
  choosing $\mc{V}$ appropriately, and they further analyzed the
  determinization for \Rel-automata using the Kleisli adjunction between $\Set$
  and \Rel.
  In this paper, we revisit Colcombet and Petrişan's analysis from a fibrational
  perspective, building on Melliès and Zeilberger's recent alternative but
  related definition of categorical automata as functors $p : \mc{Q} \to \mc{C}$
  satisfying the finitary fiber and unique lifting of factorizations property.
  In doing so, we improve the understanding of determinization in three regards:
  Firstly, we carefully describe the universal property of determinization in
  terms of forward-backward simulations.
  Secondly, we generalize the determinization procedure for \Rel automata using a
  local adjunction between \SpanSet and \Rel, which provides us with a
  canonical forward simulation.
  Finally we also propose an alterative determinization based on the multiset
  relative adjunction which retains paths, and we leverage this to provide
  a canonical forward-backward simulation.

  % % Intro
  % In this paper, we study determinization categorically for
  % \Rel-automata and ULF-automata.
  % % What you do for Rel
  % We give a detailed presentation of the determinization procedure
  % for \Rel-automata, as well as its correctness and universal property as
  % previously investigated by Colcombet and Petrişan.
  % Doing so we generalize its universal property using simulations.
  % % Span Set
  % We then generalize the procedure to ULF-automata using the fact that there is
  % a local adjunction between \SpanSet and \Rel, prove its correctness and
  % recover a similar universal property.
  % % Span Fin
  % This determinization procedure has the particularly to identifies the paths
  % taken, as this may not be wanted, we provide an alternative determinization
  % based on multisets that retain them.
  % Moreover, we leverage the path relevance of this determinization to provide a
  % stronger universal property.
\end{abstract}

\subimport{}{intro.tex}
\subimport{}{preliminaries.tex}
\subimport{}{rel_determinization.tex}
\subimport{}{determinization.tex}
\subimport{}{alternative.tex}

\section{Conclusion}

% \todot{Past tense}

% % Intro what you do : determinastion
% \todot{ok but clumsy} \\
In this paper, we studied determinizations for \Rel-automata and ULF-automata
from a categorical point of view. Determinization for \Rel-automata has
previously been investigated by Colcombet and Petrişan in \cite{colcombet},
as ULF-automata is a generalization of \Rel-automata, through the \SpanSet
intermediary, we consider how it might be generalized to \SpanSet-automata and
thus also to ULF-automata.

% \todot{say it was described briefly + use of adjunction}
% Detailled what was previosuly briefly before
To do so, we gave a detailed presentation of the determinization procedure for \Rel, which
relies on the Kleisli adjunction between $\Set$ and \Rel, as
well as its correctness and universal property as previously described by
Colcombet and Petrişan.
% \todot{unclear}
% We have then generalized using simulations, better as...
% Doing so, we have generalized the universal property to simulations of automata,
% which subsumes the morphism of automata defined in \cite{colcombet},
% as simulations are simpler and more practical to work with.

% Why it is hard
Generalizing this construction to \SpanSet turned out to be non-trivial, as we
neither have an adjunction between \SpanSet and $\Set$, nor one between \SpanSet
and \Rel.
Moreover, as \SpanSet-automata corresponds to pseudofunctors, it forced us to
work in a 2-categorical setting and adapt definitions to it.
% How we proceed
We have shown that it is possible to recover a fitting determinization procedure
by first turning a pseudo-functor $\mc{C} \to \SpanSet$ into $\mc{C} \to \Rel$
using the local adjunction and between \SpanSet and \Rel then using the
\Rel-determinization to get back a functor $\mc{C} \to \Set$.
% Local adjunction get back a good UP
To prove correctness and equip the procedure with a similar universal
property we used the local adjunction and the correctness and universal
property for \Rel-determinization.
% It generalizes the usual algo
% \todot{says nothing}
The determinized automaton we obtained has subsets of states as states and a
transition function, which given a letter maps a subset to the set of
reachable states in the original automaton, analogously to
usual algorithmic determinization procedure.
We showed that our procedure does not always get whole powerset as the set of
states, but does as soon as we consider automata with a free monoid as codomain.

% headline : path irrelevant -> multisets
This determinization procedure has the particularity to identify the paths
taken, as this may not be wanted, we provided an alternative determinization
based on the multiset relative monad that retains them.
% No longer Rel
Compared to the one before, we no longer rely on the \Rel determinization
procedure as a middle step, as factoring through \Rel identifies paths.
% How
Instead, we use the relative Kleisli category of the relative multiset monad $M$,
$\Fin_M$, as a middle step, and then postcompose by the functor from
$\Fin_M$ to $\Set$ provided by Kleisli construction of  $\Fin_M$.
% Say why correct + UP
Moreover, we have proven the correctness of this procedure and provided it with
a universal property, leveraging the relative monad and that there is a local
equivalence of categories between $\Fin_M$ and \SpanFin.
% Ccl, Stronger UP
The multiset determinization enabled us to achieve path relevance, and to obtain
a stronger universal property that encompasses forward-backward simulation, as
we now get a canonical forward-backward simulation between an automaton $F$ and
its multiset determinization MDet($F$).

% \todot{not bad, to work on} \\
% Further work, like minimisation or whatever
There are many potential ways to expand the work on categorical automata, one
potential possibility is to explore the multiset determinization from the point
of view of transducers where path relevance might be desired as we in
addition to the input also have an output to take into account.
Another possibility would be minimization, as this has been done for
\Rel-automata, we might consider how to generalize this to to our setting.

\section*{Acknowledgements}
The work for this paper was done during an summer internship under the
supervision of Noam Zeilberger, the author would thus like to thank him for the
topic suggestion and many fruitful discussions. The author is also grateful to
Thomas Lamiaux for his advice on writing and his comments on
the many drafts that this paper has gone through. Finally, the author would like
to thank the anonymous reviewers for their comments and suggestions.


